WEBVTT

00:00:01.210 --> 00:00:04.505
If you want to know why people miss
library appointments, who would you

00:00:04.510 --> 00:00:07.610
interview? It’s tempting to start with
the people who show up.

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:11.810
But that’s like asking only passengers
who caught the bus why others missed it.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:14.170
Start with the decision you need to make.

00:00:14.750 --> 00:00:18.145
A library team reviewing appointment
reminders might talk to people who booked

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:22.705
and attended, people who booked and
missed, and people who tried to book and

00:00:22.710 --> 00:00:25.710
gave up. Those groups can reveal
different problems.

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:28.390
Use a short, neutral screener.

00:00:28.830 --> 00:00:30.625
Ask what happened the last time they
tried to book.

00:00:30.630 --> 00:00:34.330
Then track who was invited and who
actually spoke with you.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.205
If every interview came from the
library’s email list, ask which

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:39.950
experiences that list missed.

00:00:40.810 --> 00:00:44.510
A trusted community partner may be able
to share a voluntary invitation.

00:00:44.990 --> 00:00:47.290
Need help planning a study around the
right voices?

00:00:48.030 --> 00:00:50.425
Visit prcimpact.com or call

00:00:50.430 --> 00:00:54.450
(334) 429-6994.
